diagnostics/trait-syntax.em:2:1: a trait needs no `@abstract`
  @abstract
  ^^^^^^^^^
A trait is never constructed, and a member written without a body is already a requirement. Remove `@abstract`.
diagnostics/trait-syntax.em:3:13: a trait builds on other traits with `with`
  trait Shape extends Base {
              ^^^^^^^
Write `with` in place of `extends`, as in `trait Pet with Named`. A trait cannot extend a class.
diagnostics/trait-syntax.em:7:11: a trait stores nothing, so `value` cannot have a value here
      const value: Int = 3
            ^^^^^
Leave out `= ...` so each type that adopts the trait supplies `value`, or give it a body that computes it.
diagnostics/trait-syntax.em:9:5: a trait has no constructor
      constructor() {
      ^^^^^^^^^^^
A trait stores nothing to set up. Each type that adopts it builds its own values.
diagnostics/trait-syntax.em:12:5: a trait has no type-level members
      func Stored.make() {
      ^^^^
Declare the type-level member in each type that adopts the trait.
diagnostics/trait-syntax.em:15:5: a trait's requirements need no `@abstract`
      @abstract
      ^^^^^^^^^
Leave out the body instead: a member of a trait written without one is a requirement.
diagnostics/trait-syntax.em:19:16: a trait has no base class, so there is no `super`
          return super.perimeter()
                 ^^^^^
To run the default of a trait this one builds on, write `Trait.name(self)`.
diagnostics/trait-syntax.em:24:5: a struct has nothing to override
      @override
      ^^^^^^^^^
Structs do not inherit, and this one adopts no traits. Remove the annotation, or adopt the trait with `with`.
